"""100 distinct owner questions researched October 6, 2026.

Search demand was identified from IRS FAQ collections and public discussions.
Tax positions rely on the primary sources linked in each article, not forum advice.
The entries below are editorial copy, not random permutations of keywords.
"""
GROUPS=[
('S-Corp Planning','https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/s-corporation-compensation-and-medical-insurance-issues','compensation',[
('Can I use a 60/40 salary split for my S corporation?', 'There is no universal IRS-approved 60/40 split. Reasonable compensation depends on the services you perform, the time you spend, comparable pay and the source of the company earnings. A percentage can be a modeling assumption, but it is not a substitute for a compensation analysis.','Compare an owner performing all billable work with an owner whose employees produce most revenue. The same profit can support different pay conclusions.'),
('Should my S corporation salary change when profit doubles?', 'Revisit the salary analysis, but do not mechanically double wages. Profit growth can come from more owner labor, additional employees, pricing changes or invested capital. The payroll decision should follow the work the shareholder actually performs and the compensation evidence for that role.','A contractor adds two crews while the owner keeps the same supervisory duties. Document the new organization before changing the salary.'),
('Can I take S corporation distributions before running owner payroll?', 'An owner who provides services cannot use distributions as a substitute for reasonable compensation. Payment labels do not control the federal treatment. If payroll has been omitted, have the preparer and payroll provider evaluate the payments, timing and required corrections before making more distributions.','A shareholder takes monthly draws but has no W-2 wages. Reconcile the payments instead of simply renaming the last draw a salary.'),
('Does a part-time S corporation owner need reasonable compensation?', 'Part-time work does not eliminate the compensation requirement. The relevant question is the value of the services actually provided, not whether the business is the owner\'s main occupation. Document hours, duties, revenue-generating work and who performs the remaining functions.','An owner spends ten hours a week managing a consulting team while holding another job. A full-time comparator needs adjustment for the actual duties.'),
('Do two S corporation owners have to earn identical salaries?', 'Not necessarily. Wages compensate services, while distributions reflect ownership rights and the S corporation rules. Owners with different duties or hours can have different reasonable wages. The wage policy should be supported, and the distribution arrangement should be reviewed separately for tax classification concerns.','One shareholder sells and manages clients; the other handles occasional administration. Equal stock ownership does not establish equal labor value.'),
('Can an S corporation owner pay zero salary in a loss year?', 'A loss year does not create a blanket permission to ignore shareholder compensation. Review the services, payments, available cash and whether the corporation paid personal expenses or made other transfers. The analysis is more specific than comparing year-end net income with zero.','A company reports a tax loss after accelerated depreciation but distributes cash. The noncash deduction does not resolve the wage question.'),
('Should a real estate agent elect S corporation status after a strong commission year?', 'Compare expected recurring net profit with reasonable wages, payroll costs, state taxes and compliance duties. A single commission spike can exaggerate the ongoing benefit. Also distinguish the commission business from rental ownership; the same tax classification need not suit both activities.','An agent closes one unusually large transaction. Model the following year using repeatable earnings before committing to additional administration.'),
('Does paying myself more S corporation wages always reduce my total tax?', 'No. Higher wages can affect payroll taxes, retirement contribution capacity and other deductions in different directions. Start with a defensible reasonable compensation amount, then model the combined business and household result. The lowest income tax number alone is not a complete comparison.','An owner raises salary to support retirement funding. Compare the additional payroll cost with the actual contribution and other tax effects.'),
('Can an S corporation pay a shareholder health insurance premium?', 'It can, but a more-than-2% shareholder has special treatment. Eligible premiums paid or reimbursed by the corporation are generally included in the shareholder\'s Box 1 wages, with other payroll treatment depending on the arrangement. The shareholder deduction has separate requirements, including employer-plan eligibility restrictions.','A shareholder pays premiums personally. Ask whether the corporation established the coverage through payment or reimbursement and reported it correctly.'),
('What evidence should I keep for an S corporation salary review?', 'Keep a description of duties, hours, qualifications, comparable compensation, staffing and how revenue is generated. Retain the reasoning behind the selected wage and update it when the work changes. A payroll number without supporting facts is less useful than a documented, reproducible decision.','A practice owner combines clinical care, management and supervision. Explain each role instead of comparing only one job title.'),
]),
('Tax Compliance','https://www.irs.gov/publications/p505','review',[
('How should a business owner pay estimated tax on uneven income?', 'Estimate the household tax and consider the annualized income installment method when income arrives unevenly. Equal payments based on an annual forecast are not the only possible approach. Timing matters, so keep period-by-period income and deduction records rather than reconstructing them after filing.','A brokerage earns most of its commission in the fourth quarter. A dated profit report can support a different installment analysis from evenly earned income.'),
('Does an estimated tax safe harbor mean I will not owe more in April?', 'No. A safe harbor addresses the underpayment penalty, not the final tax bill. A payment pattern can satisfy a safe harbor while leaving a substantial balance due. Calculate both the penalty-protection amount and the projected remaining liability so the cash reserve is realistic.','A profitable business uses last year\'s tax to set installments. Current earnings rise sharply, leaving a larger final bill even if payments were timely.'),
('Can spouse W-2 withholding cover tax on my business income?', 'On a joint return, household withholding can help cover the combined tax liability. Review the entire return, withholding timing and applicable estimated-tax rules. The business still needs a cash plan, and state obligations may differ from the federal calculation.','One spouse has a salaried job and the other owns an LLC. Review additional withholding alongside the LLC profit rather than treating the two incomes separately.'),
('Do I owe personal estimated tax when my S corporation keeps the cash?', 'Pass-through income can be taxable to the shareholder even without a distribution. Corporate cash retention and the shareholder tax liability are different questions. Review K-1 income, wages, withholding and the distribution policy before assuming no cash received means no tax due.','A company retains earnings to hire staff. The shareholder may still need funds outside the company for tax payments.'),
('Should I reserve tax money from revenue or from profit?', 'Profit and the household tax projection are the better starting points, with adjustments for book-tax differences and other income. A flat percentage of revenue can misstate the need when margins vary. Reconcile the books and update the reserve as earnings change.','Two businesses each collect the same revenue but have different labor and materials costs. Their owner tax reserves need not match.'),
('Can I fix a missed estimated tax payment by paying extra later?', 'A later payment can reduce an outstanding balance, but it does not automatically erase an earlier period\'s underpayment. The penalty calculation considers installment timing and available exceptions. Ask the preparer to evaluate annualization, withholding and the actual payment dates.','An owner skips the first installment and pays twice as much later. Preserve confirmation dates so the preparer can compute the correct result.'),
('Does extending my business return extend my tax payment deadline?', 'An extension to file generally does not extend the time to pay tax due. Identify which entity and individual returns require estimated balances, then arrange payment by the applicable original deadline. A later K-1 can require an updated estimate without changing that distinction.','A partnership return is extended, delaying a shareholder\'s or partner\'s information. The household still needs an estimated payment calculation.'),
('How do rental property sales affect quarterly estimated taxes?', 'A sale can create tax from gain, depreciation-related amounts and other applicable items even when much of the proceeds repay a loan. Update the household projection using adjusted basis and transaction costs. For uneven income, review whether annualization is appropriate.','An investor receives little cash after mortgage payoff. The cash settlement alone does not measure taxable gain.'),
('Can I use last year\'s refund toward this year\'s business taxes?', 'An overpayment may be applied to the following year when properly elected on the return. Verify the amount actually applied and how it is credited to estimated payments. Do not count the same dollars as both a cash refund and an installment credit.','A preparer elects to carry an overpayment forward. Reconcile the return election with the IRS account before reducing new payments.'),
('What should an owner include in a midyear tax projection?', 'Include operating income, wages, spouse income, rentals, investment income, prior carryforwards, deductions and payments already made. Separate book depreciation from tax depreciation and cash withdrawals from taxable income. The projection should produce both a tax estimate and a cash funding schedule.','An owner has two S corporations and three rentals. A single company profit report cannot represent the complete household tax position.'),
]),
('Business Deductions','https://www.irs.gov/publications/p463','reimbursements',[
('Can I reimburse owner business expenses paid with a personal credit card?', 'The payment method does not decide deductibility. A corporation can reimburse qualifying, substantiated business expenses under a proper accountable arrangement. The receipt, business purpose and repayment of excess advances matter. Prevent a second deduction on the personal return or company ledger.','An owner pays for a business software subscription personally. Submit the invoice and business purpose, then reconcile the corporate reimbursement.'),
('Does an accountable plan let me deduct all my personal meals?', 'No. A reimbursement policy cannot change a personal meal into a business expense. The underlying meal must qualify, be substantiated and remain subject to applicable limitations. Record who attended, the business purpose and the amount rather than relying on the card statement alone.','A restaurant charge appears on an owner expense report with no attendee or purpose details. The policy itself does not fill that evidence gap.'),
('Can my company reimburse mileage if I also use the car personally?', 'Business miles can potentially be reimbursed when adequately documented; personal use does not make every mile deductible. Separate qualifying business travel from commuting and personal trips. Apply the relevant year\'s method and avoid combining incompatible vehicle expense treatments.','A contractor drives from a jobsite to a supplier and later to a personal appointment. The mileage log should distinguish those legs.'),
('How should I document a trip that mixes business and vacation?', 'Keep the itinerary, dates, business activities, receipts and personal portions. The treatment depends on travel location and the role of the business purpose, with special rules for certain trips. Do not assume one meeting makes every hotel night or family ticket deductible.','An owner attends a conference and stays longer with family. Separate the conference period and personal extension before preparing the expense report.'),
('Can I deduct commuting when I own the business?', 'Ownership alone does not turn commuting into business travel. Transportation between home and a regular workplace is generally personal, subject to specific exceptions. Review your work locations and any qualifying home office before choosing a mileage treatment.','A salon owner drives daily from home to the same salon. That routine trip is different from travel between business locations.'),
('Can I reimburse expenses without keeping paper receipts?', 'Electronic records can be useful if they preserve the necessary details and remain accessible. A bank charge by itself may not establish what was purchased or why it was business-related. Keep itemized documentation and required substantiation, with applicable exceptions evaluated by the preparer.','A digital hotel invoice and dated meeting agenda provide more context than a credit card transaction labeled with the hotel name.'),
('How should a business owner handle reimbursement advances?', 'Under an accountable arrangement, the employee must substantiate qualifying expenses and return excess advances within a reasonable period. An advance is not permission to retain unused cash tax-free. Set deadlines, reconcile the advance and identify any amount requiring payroll treatment.','A company advances travel money but the trip costs less. The unused balance should be returned and the reconciliation retained.'),
('Can I deduct travel to inspect a possible rental property?', 'Treatment depends on the facts, including whether you have an existing activity and whether the spending relates to acquiring a property or conducting operations. Do not automatically classify every scouting trip as a current deduction. Keep property-specific notes and ask whether costs must be capitalized or otherwise limited.','An owner visits several properties before choosing one. Track the purpose and costs separately from family travel and later operating expenses.'),
('Can my company pay for my spouse to accompany me on business travel?', 'A spouse\'s travel is not deductible merely because the owner\'s trip is business-related. Employee status, a bona fide business purpose and the other applicable requirements matter. Social companionship or informal help does not automatically satisfy them.','A spouse attends dinners during a conference but has no documented company role. Review their expenses separately from the owner\'s.'),
('What should I do when an owner expense report includes personal charges?', 'Remove or separately classify personal amounts, then reconcile whether they were repaid or treated as compensation, a distribution or another appropriate item. The classification depends on the entity and facts. Do not bury personal charges in a general business expense account.','A personal grocery purchase appears beside business supplies. Correct the ledger and reimbursement report before relying on the annual profit number.'),
]),
('Retirement Planning','https://www.irs.gov/publications/p560','retirement',[
('Can S corporation distributions fund my 401(k) contribution limit?', 'S corporation distributions are not compensation for retirement plan contribution calculations. Eligible W-2 compensation matters for the shareholder-employee. Cash from a distribution may provide liquidity, but it does not create contribution capacity by itself. Check the actual plan and annual limits.','An owner takes modest wages and large distributions. The distribution total cannot simply be substituted for plan compensation.'),
('Can I keep a solo 401(k) after hiring employees?', 'An owner-only plan must be reevaluated when common-law employees become eligible. You cannot ignore employee participation requirements just because the plan began as a solo arrangement. Review eligibility, service, hours and plan terms with the administrator before continuing contributions.','A consultant hires an assistant. The hire date and service record belong in the plan review, not just the payroll file.'),
('Should a cash balance plan be based on one unusually profitable year?', 'Evaluate several years of funding capacity and employee costs. A cash balance plan is a defined benefit arrangement with actuarial and ongoing obligations. A contribution estimate based on a strong year is not proof that the plan fits the business\'s long-term cash position.','A contractor has one exceptional project and uncertain future margins. Compare sustainable funding with the projected deduction before adoption.'),
('Do employees change how much my retirement plan will cost?', 'Yes. Eligibility, compensation, age and required coverage can materially affect employer contributions and plan design. Ask for the entire company cost, including employee funding and administration, rather than only the owner contribution. Related businesses may need to be considered together.','A practice owner receives an attractive personal contribution estimate. Add staff funding and annual plan fees to the comparison.'),
('Can my spouse participate in my business retirement plan?', 'A spouse who genuinely works for the business and has eligible compensation may participate under the plan terms. Ownership or marriage alone is not a substitute for real employment. Document services and compensation, then review contribution limits and any other plans.','A spouse manages scheduling and bookkeeping. Keep the job description and payroll records alongside the employee census.'),
('Do two businesses give me two separate employee deferral limits?', 'Multiple businesses do not automatically multiply an individual\'s elective deferral limit. Aggregate applicable deferrals and review related-employer rules and employer contribution limits separately. The ownership relationships and plan types are central to the analysis.','An owner has one plan through an S corporation and another through a second activity. The administrator needs both records before approving contributions.'),
('Can rental income support a solo 401(k) contribution?', 'Ordinary investment rental income generally is not self-employment earned income for retirement plan purposes. A property-related operating business may have different facts. Review the activity and qualifying compensation rather than treating every Schedule E profit as plan income.','An investor has rental cash flow but no wages or qualifying self-employment earnings. Cash availability alone does not establish eligibility.'),
('Is a deductible retirement contribution the same as tax-free retirement income?', 'No. A current deduction and future distribution treatment are different questions. Pretax contributions generally defer taxation, while Roth arrangements have different contribution and withdrawal rules. Compare the current tax benefit, future tax treatment and liquidity restrictions before selecting a contribution type.','An owner assumes a deducted contribution will also be tax-free when withdrawn. The plan type and distribution rules must be reviewed.'),
('What should I do after contributing too much to a business retirement plan?', 'Identify the type of excess, the applicable plan and the year before moving money. Excess elective deferrals, annual additions and nondeductible employer contributions can require different corrections. Work with the administrator and preparer promptly; an ordinary withdrawal may not fix the problem.','An owner combines contributions from two plans and discovers an excess. Gather both statements and payroll records before requesting a correction.'),
('What records should I give an actuary for a cash balance plan proposal?', 'Provide ownership across businesses, employee census, compensation, dates of birth, service records, existing plans and a multi-year cash forecast. A reliable design needs the full employer picture. Ask the actuary to show owner benefits, staff costs, funding obligations and termination considerations.','An owner submits only personal age and profit. The missing staff and related-business facts can change the proposal materially.'),
]),
('Entity Structuring','https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/s-corporation-stock-and-debt-basis','review',[
('Does guaranteeing my S corporation bank loan create debt basis?', 'A guarantee alone generally does not create shareholder debt basis. Bona fide indebtedness running directly from the corporation to the shareholder is central to the debt-basis analysis. If the guarantor actually pays, review the resulting facts rather than assuming the original guarantee was enough.','A company borrows from a bank with an owner guarantee. Keep that separate from a documented loan the owner actually makes to the company.'),
('Can debt basis make an S corporation distribution tax-free?', 'Debt basis and stock basis have different roles. Nondividend distributions generally use stock basis, while qualifying debt basis may support loss deductions after stock basis is exhausted. Do not combine the balances into one pool for distribution planning.','An owner has little stock basis but a large direct company loan. That loan balance does not automatically shelter a cash distribution.'),
('Why can repayment of my shareholder loan create taxable income?', 'If prior losses reduced the tax basis in the loan, repayment can create a taxable amount. Face value and adjusted debt basis are not always equal. Reconcile the loan\'s tax history and any restoration before deciding that all principal repayment is tax-free.','A shareholder lent cash, deducted losses against debt basis and later receives repayment. The original amount advanced is not the only number needed.'),
('Can a bookkeeping loan entry cure an excess S corporation distribution?', 'A label alone does not establish a bona fide loan. The actual transaction, repayment intent, documentation and economic facts must support loan treatment. Review excess distributions directly rather than assuming an accounting reclassification erases their tax consequences.','An owner withdraws cash and the bookkeeper posts it to shareholder receivables at year-end. A journal entry does not establish the full legal and tax facts.'),
('Can I deduct my entire K-1 loss if the business lost money?', 'Not automatically. Shareholder basis, at-risk rules, passive activity limits and excess business loss rules may restrict a current deduction. A K-1 reports the allocated item; it does not by itself establish the amount currently deductible on the individual return.','A company reports a large depreciation loss. The shareholder still needs their basis and limitation schedules before using it against other income.'),
('Should I track stock basis separately for each S corporation?', 'Yes. Basis is tied to the specific investment and debt relationship. A contribution to one corporation does not automatically support losses or distributions from another. Maintain year-by-year schedules that connect to each entity\'s K-1 and actual shareholder transactions.','An owner operates two companies and moves cash between them. Identify the legal transfer instead of netting all company balances together.'),
('Do retained S corporation profits affect shareholder stock basis?', 'Allocated income generally increases stock basis, subject to the applicable ordering and adjustments. Retained cash is not itself the basis calculation. Reconcile income, distributions, nondeductible expenses and losses to understand the shareholder\'s ending basis.','A business has cash in the bank but also prior distributions and losses. The bank balance cannot replace the shareholder schedule.'),
('Can contributing cash before year-end support an S corporation loss?', 'A genuine cash contribution can increase stock basis, but timing, actual payment and other limitations still matter. A planned contribution that never occurs does not support the same position. Document the transfer and test the loss through the remaining applicable limits.','An owner plans to fund the business on December 31. Confirm the completed transaction and the relevant tax year instead of relying on an unsigned plan.'),
('What happens to S corporation losses suspended for lack of basis?', 'Basis-limited losses are generally tracked at the shareholder level for possible use when sufficient basis becomes available, subject to other applicable rules. They are not automatically lost or currently deductible. Keep the history by entity and loss item so later increases can be applied correctly.','A shareholder makes a new contribution the following year. The preparer needs the suspended-loss schedule, not just the new bank receipt.'),
('What should Form 7203 reconcile to in my owner tax file?', 'The stock and debt basis calculation should connect to contributions, distributions, income, losses, nondeductible items and shareholder loan activity. Determine whether filing is required under the form instructions. A completed form is more useful when the supporting schedules explain how each balance arose.','An owner has several years of changing distributions. Reconstruct the opening balance rather than starting the current form with an unexplained number.'),
]),
('Rental Property','https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527','assets',[
('Is a refundable rental security deposit taxable when received?', 'A deposit intended to be returned is generally not rental income when received. Amounts retained under the lease can become income when applied or kept. A payment designated as last month\'s rent is treated differently from a refundable deposit. Keep the lease and deposit ledger consistent.','A landlord receives first month\'s rent and a refundable damage deposit. The two amounts should not automatically share the same income classification.'),
('Is advance rent taxable before the tenant uses the property?', 'Advance rent is generally included in rental income when received, even if it applies to a later period or your accounting method differs. Review what the payment actually represents. A refundable deposit and a prepaid rental payment have different treatment.','A tenant prepays several months near year-end. The lease term alone does not move all of that receipt into the following tax year.'),
('Do tenant-paid expenses count as rental income?', 'When a tenant pays an owner obligation, the payment can be rental income to the owner. A corresponding deduction may be available if the underlying cost qualifies. Record both sides instead of omitting the transaction because no money passed through the landlord\'s bank account.','A tenant pays a repair bill that was the landlord\'s responsibility and reduces rent. The ledger should explain the rent and expense treatment.'),
('Can I deduct mortgage principal on a rental property?', 'Mortgage principal payments are not a rental operating deduction. Qualifying interest and depreciation are separate items with their own rules. A loan statement should distinguish principal, interest and escrow so the property ledger does not treat the entire payment as an expense.','A monthly mortgage debit includes principal, interest and tax escrow. Split those components before calculating taxable rental income.'),
('Is replacing a rental roof a repair or an improvement?', 'A roof replacement often requires capitalization as a restoration or improvement, but the facts and applicable rules control. Compare the scope of work with the property\'s relevant unit of property and consider available elections only when requirements are met. The invoice label does not decide treatment.','A landlord replaces the entire roof rather than patching a leak. Ask the preparer to assess capitalization and any disposition issues.'),
('Can I depreciate land when I buy a rental property?', 'No. Allocate the purchase basis between land and depreciable property using a supportable method. A cost segregation study can classify eligible components but does not make land depreciable. Preserve the evidence supporting the allocation rather than choosing a percentage solely to enlarge a deduction.','A purchase price covers both the lot and the house. The full closing price is not the building\'s depreciable basis.'),
('When does rental depreciation begin if renovations delay tenants?', 'Depreciation begins when the property is placed in service, meaning ready and available for its intended rental use. Closing or payment alone does not establish that date. Keep evidence of readiness, marketing, inspections and completion of work necessary for rental availability.','A property closes in December but remains uninhabitable until February. The acquisition date and service date need separate analysis.'),
('Can I deduct rental expenses while the property is vacant?', 'Ordinary costs of holding a property out for rent can generally remain deductible during a vacancy, subject to the nature of the expense and other rules. Personal use, withdrawal from rental availability and improvements require separate treatment. Retain marketing and operating records showing the rental purpose.','A tenant moves out and the owner continues advertising the unit. That is different from converting it to personal use.'),
('How does personal use of a vacation rental affect deductions?', 'Track rental days, personal-use days and expenses. Personal use can trigger allocation and special vacation-home limitations. Family use, below-market arrangements and other circumstances can count differently from an ordinary paid guest stay. Do not treat every occupied day as a business day.','The owner blocks several peak weeks for family. Reservation exports alone may not identify the personal-use limitation.'),
('Do Airbnb platform fees reduce the rent I report?', 'Reconcile gross guest charges with fees, refunds and actual deposits. A net bank deposit does not necessarily represent gross rental income. Platform charges may be deductible when they relate to the rental activity, but the ledger should show the components and avoid double counting.','A platform deposits a net amount after service charges. Compare that deposit with the reservation statement and tax reporting document.'),
]),
('Material Participation','https://www.irs.gov/publications/p925','rentals',[
('Is exactly 100 hours enough for the short-term rental participation test?', 'The commonly cited test requires more than 100 hours and participation at least as much as any other individual. Exactly 100 hours does not meet that test. Other material participation tests may apply, but each has its own requirements. First verify whether the activity falls outside the rental definition.','An owner records exactly 100 hours while a manager records 120. The owner has not satisfied the commonly discussed comparison test.'),
('Do my spouse\'s hours count toward rental material participation?', 'For material participation, a spouse\'s participation is generally taken into account even if the spouse does not own an interest or the spouses file separately. Real estate professional qualification has a separate spouse rule: on a joint return, one spouse must independently meet its qualification requirements.','A couple combines rental operating hours. Do not automatically use the combined total to satisfy one spouse\'s real estate professional qualification.'),
('Can my business partner\'s hours count as my participation?', 'A partner\'s work generally is not treated as your own participation simply because you share ownership. Spouse participation has a specific rule that unrelated partners do not share. Evaluate each owner\'s facts and the applicable participation test rather than relying on the partnership\'s total hours.','Two unrelated owners split operations. Each should maintain their own records and review the test that supports their individual tax treatment.'),
('Can I materially participate when I hire a property manager?', 'Hiring a manager does not automatically prevent material participation, but their work can matter under the applicable test. The more-than-100-hour test includes a comparison with every other individual. Different tests can produce different results, so record your substantive work and the manager\'s role.','An owner supervises vendors while a manager handles daily guest communications. Compare the actual duties and hours rather than relying on job titles.'),
('Does an average guest stay of seven days make my rental loss deductible?', 'An average customer-use period of seven days or less is an exception to the passive-activity rental definition, not a complete deduction approval. Material participation and other applicable loss limitations still need review. Depreciation alone cannot resolve these remaining questions.','A property has short stays but the owner does little operating work. The average stay calculation does not establish nonpassive treatment by itself.'),
('Does a real estate license automatically qualify me as a real estate professional?', 'No. Qualification depends on qualifying services and time, including more than 750 hours and more than half of the taxpayer\'s personal services in qualifying real property businesses. A license is not the test. Rental material participation remains a separate requirement.','An agent holds an active license but works mainly in another profession. Review actual services and competing work time.'),
('Can a full-time W-2 employee qualify as a real estate professional?', 'It is possible only if the actual facts satisfy the qualification requirements, including the more-than-half personal-services test. A substantial non-real-estate job makes that comparison important. Certain employee real estate services also have an ownership requirement before they count.','An employee reports extensive rental work while working a full-time unrelated job. The annual calendar must support both sets of claimed hours.'),
('Are investor research hours the same as rental participation hours?', 'Not necessarily. Reviewing financial statements and monitoring an investment in an investor capacity can be excluded unless connected with day-to-day management or operations under the applicable rules. Identify the work performed, not just time spent thinking about a property.','An owner logs hours comparing investment returns. Separate that activity from documented guest, maintenance or operating decisions.'),
('Can I combine every rental property to meet material participation?', 'Not automatically. Rental activity identification, permitted grouping and a real estate professional\'s election to treat rental real estate interests as one activity have specific rules. The election should not be assumed, and short-stay activities need their own classification review.','An investor adds hours from six properties without checking the prior election. The return history and activity structure need review first.'),
('Does meeting material participation release every prior suspended loss?', 'No. Current-year participation and prior-year suspended losses have separate consequences. Former passive activity rules can limit how old losses are used, and a qualifying disposition follows different rules. Track the carryforward by activity and year before treating it as a current offset.','A rental becomes nonpassive after several passive years. The new status does not automatically make all old losses deductible against wages.'),
]),
('Cost Segregation','https://www.irs.gov/publications/p946','assets',[
('Can a cost segregation report write off the entire rental purchase price?', 'No. Land remains nondepreciable, and the building structure generally retains its applicable recovery period. A study identifies components with supportable classifications and recovery periods. Bonus depreciation applies only to eligible property under the relevant acquisition and service-date rules.','An investor receives a large study deduction estimate. Compare it with the land allocation, building basis and the actual eligible components.'),
('Can I use 100% bonus depreciation on a property acquired in 2026?', 'Certain qualified property acquired and placed in service after January 19, 2025 can qualify for the restored 100% allowance. That does not mean an entire building qualifies. Review the asset class, acquisition rules, use, elections and limitations, including state treatment.','A 2026 rental purchase contains eligible short-lived components and a longer-lived structure. The asset schedule should distinguish them.'),
('Does a cost segregation deduction equal the refund I will receive?', 'No. A deduction reduces taxable income only to the extent currently usable. Tax savings depend on rates, other income, loss limitations and payments already made. A refund is the difference between liability and payments, not simply a percentage of the study estimate.','A study identifies a deduction while passive losses are suspended. The report does not establish a current cash refund.'),
('Can I order a cost segregation study several years after buying?', 'A later study can be useful, but implementation may require an accounting method change rather than simply altering the current schedule. Review prior depreciation, elections, ownership and eligibility. The correction procedure and any catch-up adjustment should be determined by the return preparer.','An owner has depreciated a rental for several years. Send the original schedule and filed returns with the study request.'),
('Should furniture already on my asset schedule appear again in a cost segregation study?', 'The study and existing depreciation register must be reconciled to prevent double counting. Identify separately purchased furniture, acquired property components and later improvements. A valid classification report still needs coordination with the return\'s asset records.','An STR owner separately depreciates new beds and couches. Those costs should not be added again as acquired building components.'),
('Does the previous owner\'s cost segregation study determine my depreciation?', 'A buyer generally establishes their own basis from the transaction, subject to applicable rules. The seller\'s adjusted basis and deductions are not simply transferred in a normal taxable purchase. Prior study information may assist classification, but the new owner\'s price and acquisition facts matter.','A seller provides an old report. Reconcile it with the buyer\'s settlement statement and asset allocation before using it.'),
('Can I elect out of bonus depreciation after receiving a cost segregation study?', 'The depreciation election and study classification are separate decisions. An election out is subject to the applicable rules, often by class of property, with timing and procedural requirements. Model current and future tax effects before treating the report as an instruction to claim every acceleration.','An owner expects much higher income next year. Review the election options and deadlines before finalizing the depreciation return.'),
('Does financing a property reduce the basis available for depreciation?', 'A purchase funded with bona fide debt generally does not limit depreciable cost to the down payment. However, land allocation, capitalized costs and the actual transaction still determine basis. At-risk and other rules can separately limit a loss deduction.','A buyer makes a small down payment and borrows the balance. Distinguish total basis from cash invested and loss limitations.'),
('Can cost segregation help if my rental losses are currently passive?', 'It may accelerate deductions, but passive limitations can postpone their use. Model future passive income, expected holding period and sale consequences before paying for a study. A larger suspended loss is not the same as an immediate reduction in household tax.','An investor has no current passive income and does not qualify for nonpassive treatment. Compare the projected timing benefit with the study cost.'),
('What should I verify before implementing a cost segregation report?', 'Reconcile the study with the closing statement, land allocation, service date, asset register, improvements and filed depreciation. Review eligible bonus treatment, state adjustments and loss usability with the preparer. The report should support a coordinated tax schedule, not stand alone as a deduction promise.','An owner receives a completed report just before filing. A short reconciliation checklist can prevent basis and furniture duplication errors.'),
]),
('1031 Exchanges','https://www.irs.gov/publications/p544','sale',[
('Can I receive rental sale proceeds and arrange a 1031 exchange afterward?', 'Actual or constructive receipt of sale proceeds can prevent a qualifying deferred exchange. Arrange the structure before closing with a qualified intermediary and transaction advisers. Buying another property later does not automatically turn an ordinary sale into an exchange.','A seller directs the proceeds to a personal account and then contacts an intermediary. The receipt of funds is a central problem.'),
('Does paying off my mortgage eliminate tax on a rental sale?', 'No. Gain is generally based on amount realized and adjusted basis, not the cash left after paying the mortgage. Debt payoff affects settlement cash and can also matter in an exchange analysis. Prepare a tax calculation separately from the closing cash estimate.','A seller has substantial debt and little net cash. That does not establish a small taxable gain.'),
('Can I exchange real estate for business equipment under Section 1031?', 'Current Section 1031 treatment generally applies to qualifying real property, not equipment or other personal property. A business purchase can include several asset types, which must be analyzed separately. Do not apply the real estate exchange rules to the entire transaction by default.','An owner sells a warehouse with machinery. The machinery and real estate need distinct tax treatment.'),
('Can a house bought primarily to flip qualify for a 1031 exchange?', 'Property held primarily for sale does not qualify merely because it is real estate. Investment purpose, actual use and the surrounding business facts matter. An arbitrary holding period or an LLC label does not automatically convert inventory into investment property.','A developer buys, renovates and markets a house for resale. Review that purpose before considering exchange treatment.'),
('Does a 1031 exchange permanently erase depreciation recapture?', 'An eligible exchange can defer gain, including depreciation-related amounts under applicable rules, but it does not automatically erase the tax history. Replacement property basis and future disposition remain important. Cash, nonqualifying property or other transaction features can create current recognition.','An investor exchanges a depreciated rental and later sells the replacement outright. The deferred tax history remains relevant.'),
('Can I buy replacement property in a different LLC?', 'The tax identity of the seller and replacement-property owner matters. A disregarded LLC can be different from a separate tax-paying partnership or corporation. Review ownership and classification before changing the title holder, especially when new partners are involved.','An individual sells and proposes buying through a new multi-member LLC. The legal name alone does not resolve the taxpayer continuity question.'),
('What happens if I keep some cash in a 1031 exchange?', 'Cash retained can produce recognized gain, limited and characterized under the applicable rules. Debt changes and nonqualifying property can also affect the calculation. An exchange may still defer part of the gain, so compare the full transaction rather than treating it as all-or-nothing.','A seller keeps cash for personal spending while buying a replacement. The retained amount belongs in the tax model.'),
('Do 1031 exchange deadlines pause while I negotiate financing?', 'The identification and exchange completion periods are strict, subject to specific exceptions such as applicable disaster relief. Financing delays do not generally extend them. Plan lender work, due diligence and backups before the clock becomes the main constraint.','An investor identifies a property but cannot close on time. An ongoing loan discussion does not by itself change the deadline.'),
('How do I calculate adjusted basis before selling a rental?', 'Begin with acquisition basis, then reconcile capital improvements, depreciation allowed or allowable and other adjustments. Missing depreciation records can misstate gain. Obtain the full historical schedule rather than subtracting only deductions remembered from the last return.','A property has been refinanced and renovated several times. The current loan balance is not the adjusted tax basis.'),
('Should I choose a 1031 exchange solely to avoid this year\'s tax?', 'Compare deferral with replacement-property economics, financing, fees, liquidity and future tax exposure. A poor investment does not become attractive solely because it preserves a deferral. The transaction should meet both tax requirements and the owner\'s actual portfolio objectives.','An investor considers an overpriced replacement near the deadline. Compare the after-tax sale alternative before committing.'),
]),
('Tax Return Mistakes','https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i3115','review',[
('Is Form 3115 always required when depreciation was missed?', 'No. The correct procedure depends on what happened, how many returns are involved and whether a method of accounting has been adopted. Some errors use amended returns, while qualifying method changes can use Form 3115. Establish the filing history before selecting a form.','A property was omitted on one return versus depreciated incorrectly for several years. The same missing deduction description can hide different procedures.'),
('Can I put all missed depreciation on this year\'s Schedule E?', 'Do not simply add a prior-year total to current depreciation. Determine whether an amended return or accounting method change is appropriate and calculate any applicable adjustment. The current asset schedule and the prior-year treatment must be reconciled.','An owner discovers several years of missed depreciation. A single additional expense line does not establish the proper correction.'),
('Does a new cost segregation report require amending every old return?', 'Not necessarily. A qualifying change in depreciation method may be implemented through Form 3115 with a Section 481(a) adjustment, while other situations require different treatment. Review eligibility, automatic-change rules, elections and the prior schedules before deciding the filing route.','A property has an established depreciation history. The preparer should choose the procedure before promising several amended refunds.'),
('What is a Section 481(a) adjustment supposed to reconcile?', 'It accounts for differences caused by an accounting method change so amounts are not duplicated or omitted across tax years. In depreciation work, the computation compares the relevant prior treatment with the proposed correct method. It is not a separate deduction invented by the study provider.','The preparer compares cumulative depreciation under two methods. The adjustment needs to connect to the actual asset history.'),
('Can I claim a catch-up deduction after selling the rental?', 'Disposition can change eligibility and the applicable procedure. Certain accounting method changes have special rules for disposed property, and the year of sale matters. Review the current revenue procedure and facts before assuming a post-sale deduction is available.','An owner orders a study after closing the sale. The transaction date and filed return history become especially important.'),
('Can Form 3115 fix a missed bonus depreciation election automatically?', 'A method change and an election correction are different procedural questions. A missed or incorrect election may need separate relief and is not automatically solved by filing Form 3115. Review the specific election, timing, return and available relief provisions.','An owner elected out of bonus depreciation and later regrets the result. That is different from assigning an asset the wrong recovery period.'),
('What records should I give my CPA for a depreciation correction?', 'Provide acquisition documents, service dates, every relevant depreciation schedule, improvement records, study reports and filed returns. Include disposals and elections. Without those records, the preparer cannot reliably compare prior deductions with the proposed method or prevent duplicate amounts.','The owner has only the newest asset report. Request the earlier schedules before agreeing to a catch-up number.'),
('Does a depreciation correction guarantee a refund?', 'No. The procedure determines how an adjustment is reported, but the tax benefit still depends on income, limitations, timing and payments. A current deduction can be suspended or produce a different result from an amended refund. Model the actual return effect before quoting cash recovery.','A catch-up adjustment creates a passive rental loss. The amount may carry forward instead of producing a current refund.'),
('Can I file a depreciation method change without coordinating entity returns?', 'Entity ownership and where the original method appears determine who files and how the owner receives the tax items. A partnership-owned property is not corrected simply by changing a partner\'s individual Schedule E. Coordinate entity and owner reporting before implementation.','A multi-member LLC owns the rental. The individual owner should not independently replace the entity depreciation schedule.'),
('How do I avoid counting a catch-up deduction twice?', 'Reconcile the method-change adjustment with the updated asset register and the current depreciation computation. Identify which amounts relate to prior years and which relate to the current year. Keep the adjustment workpaper connected to the final return package.','A preparer imports a new study and also enters a catch-up amount. Check whether the import already includes the same prior-year difference.'),
]),
]

# Focused editorial context per cluster; no invented client outcomes or results.
CONTEXT={
'S-Corp Planning':('Separate wages from ownership returns','The compensation decision should come before optimizing distributions or contribution amounts. Separate what you earn for work from what the ownership investment produces. Document changes in staffing, owner duties and time; then compare federal income tax, employment taxes, state obligations and administration. A salary model should be reproducible from evidence rather than chosen to reach a preferred tax result.','Payroll registers, shareholder payment detail, job descriptions, comparable compensation and ownership records.'),
'Tax Compliance':('Separate the tax bill from the payment schedule','An accurate tax projection and a timely payment pattern solve different problems. Reconcile income from all activities, withholding and payments already made. Review federal and state installments separately and update the projection after material changes. A reserve policy needs to cover the final liability even when the payment schedule already protects against an underpayment penalty.','Current profit reports, prior returns, withholding statements, payment confirmations and dated income records.'),
'Business Deductions':('Begin with the underlying business purpose','An expense policy is a recordkeeping process, not a new category of deductible spending. Identify the business purpose and any personal component first, then select the appropriate reimbursement and reporting treatment. Keep payment records connected to itemized support, and assign responsibility for reviewing expense reports so mistakes do not accumulate until annual filing.','Itemized receipts, business-purpose notes, travel itineraries, mileage logs and the reimbursement policy.'),
'Retirement Planning':('Compare total plan cost with sustainable funding','A contribution target is meaningful only when the employer can meet the actual plan obligations. Review employee eligibility, related businesses, plan compensation and contributions already made elsewhere. Ask the administrator to show the owner benefit and the full staff and administration cost. The decision should fit expected cash flow across several years, not just an isolated deduction estimate.','Employee census, compensation, ownership across companies, existing plans and a multi-year cash forecast.'),
'Entity Structuring':('Maintain a tax basis schedule outside the bookkeeping ledger','Stock basis, debt basis, book equity and the loan face amount are different measurements. Maintain a year-by-year bridge from the opening tax balances through actual transactions and K-1 items. If a loss or distribution is proposed, test the relevant limitation before funds move. Clear documentation is particularly important when owners advance money, guarantee debt or move cash among companies.','Prior basis schedules, K-1s, contributions, distributions, loan agreements and actual payment records.'),
'Rental Property':('Keep a property ledger that reflects the actual use','Separate gross receipts, operating expenses, capital improvements, loan principal and owner spending. Track availability for rent and any personal use; these facts can affect both depreciation and current deductions. A property-level ledger makes the tax treatment easier to explain and prevents a bank deposit or mortgage debit from being mistaken for the correct tax number.','Lease agreements, reservation statements, closing documents, property ledgers and improvement invoices.'),
'Material Participation':('Test the activity before claiming the loss','First classify the activity for passive-loss purposes, then identify the specific qualification or participation test. A short average stay, a professional license and a cost segregation report answer different questions. Maintain reasonable records of actual work and reconcile them with other commitments. Basis, at-risk and excess business loss limits can still matter after passive treatment is resolved.','Participation records, guest-use periods, property manager duties, other work calendars and prior elections.'),
'Cost Segregation':('Distinguish an asset deduction from an available tax benefit','A study classifies property components; the tax return determines their treatment and current usability. Reconcile purchase basis, land, separate assets, improvements and service dates before modeling accelerated deductions. Compare study cost and timing benefits with state adjustments and future disposition consequences. The largest identified deduction is not necessarily the best planning outcome.','Closing statements, land support, existing asset schedules, service-date evidence and the study report.'),
'1031 Exchanges':('Prepare the transaction before committing to the closing structure','An exchange is a transaction structure with eligibility, taxpayer identity, receipt-of-funds and timing requirements. Build an adjusted-basis calculation alongside the cash settlement estimate. Ask the intermediary, closing professionals and tax adviser to coordinate before signing or redirecting proceeds. Then compare the replacement investment with the after-tax sale alternative.','Purchase basis, depreciation history, sale terms, intermediary documents and replacement-property ownership.'),
'Tax Return Mistakes':('Choose the correction procedure from the filing history','A correction begins with the original returns and asset schedules. Identify whether the issue is a factual error, an established method or an election before choosing the form. Reconcile all affected years and entities and check the current procedural guidance. The expected benefit should be calculated from the corrected returns and remaining loss limitations, not assumed from the size of the discovered deduction.','Filed returns, depreciation history, service dates, elections, improvement costs and disposition records.'),
}
